"Stand up and take one slow step. It feels like the easiest thing in the world. But watch closely: as you step, you lean forward and begin to fall. Then your front foot lands and catches you. Walking is falling forward and catching yourself, over and over. It took you about a year of practice as a baby just to begin, and by about the age of three most children walk the way adults do. Each foot has 26 bones. Together your two feet hold 52 of the 206 bones in your body: that is about one quarter! Seven bones make up the back half of the foot, including the heel bone, which is the largest. Five long bones run through the middle, and fourteen small ones make the toes. These bones do not lie flat. They are built into an arch, like a little stone bridge, held up by strong bands called ligaments and by tendons and muscles. When your foot presses down, the arch flattens a little and stretches those bands. When you lift your foot, they spring back. Your arch is a spring that soaks up the bump of every step and gives some of the energy back. Now follow one foot through a step. First the heel lands, on a thick pad of fat. Then the whole foot rolls flat, and your body passes over the top of that leg. Next the heel lifts, the toes bend, and the big toe gives a last push. Finally the leg swings forward through the air. Each leg spends about three fifths of the time on the ground and two fifths in the air. When you walk, at least one foot is always on the ground, and for a moment in every step, both are. When you run, there is a moment when both feet are off the ground and you are flying! Your body also moves up and down. In the middle of each walking step, you rise as you pass over your straight leg, and then you drop again. The model shows this with a yellow ball. Your feet carry you thousands of steps every day, so be kind to them. Wear shoes that fit, with room for your toes to wiggle. Go barefoot on safe ground sometimes, and let your toes spread. And walk! Now measure your own step with the tool below the model, and look at the shape of your footprint.": "0311b6e8bdb6",
"The arch of your foot works like a spring.": "bb5c4f55b2f1",
