"These bones do not lie flat. They are built into an arch, like a little stone bridge, held up by strong bands called ligaments and by tendons and muscles. When your foot presses down, the arch flattens a little and stretches those bands. When you lift your foot, they spring back. Your arch is a spring that soaks up the bump of every step and gives some of the energy back.": "93c359a0494b",
"What does the arch of your foot do?": "f06039a7be7b",
"What is the difference between walking and running?": "3cd3f58b64d6",
"When you walk, at least one foot is always on the ground, and for a moment in every step, both are. When you run, there is a moment when both feet are off the ground and you are flying! Your body also moves up and down. In the middle of each walking step, you rise as you pass over your straight leg, and then you drop again. The model shows this with a yellow ball.": "8ff2cab5c42a",
"Why can walking be called “falling and catching yourself”?": "1ed18a99c9ee",
